`ADR-001-notification-architecture.md` written — all six sections in the requested order, verified on disk (209 lines).

**Decision: Redis Streams.** The rationale is constraint-driven: at ~2k events/s peak (tens of thousands worst-case at 10x), Kafka's throughput, disk retention, and in-broker exactly-once advantages don't matter, while its ops burden violates the binding constraints — 6-person team, no infra engineer, zero Kafka experience, 2-week cap, modest budget. Redis Streams reuses the production Redis instance, lands value in days, gives at-least-once via consumer-group PEL + `XACK`/`XAUTOCLAIM`, and meets the billing exactly-once requirement the only way either system actually can: idempotent handlers keyed on the stream entry ID (Kafka's transactional EOS only spans the broker, never the external email/webhook side effect — the ADR says this explicitly).

Key trade-offs recorded in Consequences: RAM-bound retention (mitigated by `MAXLEN` trimming + Postgres as system of record), exactly-once as a discipline rather than a feature, and a single-writer ceiling — with explicit revisit conditions (throughput outgrows one Redis instance, long-term replay, or a platform team) under Alternatives Considered.
